research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-87-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-88-- **The radius $\delta$ depends on $f$ and $c$ and on nothing else here**, since the proof runs the limit condition at the single value $\varepsilon = 1$. Any other positive $\varepsilon$ would do, with $M = |L| + \varepsilon$; the value $1$ is chosen only because it is available in every ordered field ([[cor-of-one-positive]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-89-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-90-- **Why this is needed.** It is the hypothesis that makes the product case of [[thm-algebra-of-function-limits]] work: to estimate $f(x)g(x) - LM$ one needs a bound on $|f(x)|$ near $c$, and the limit of $f$ supplies one only locally. The companion fact for the denominator of a quotient — a *lower* bound on $|g|$ near $c$ — is [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]].
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-91-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-92-- **The converse fails.** A function bounded on a punctured neighbourhood of $c$ need not have a limit at $c$: the oscillator $\psi(1/x)$ on the companion page takes only values in $[0, 1/2]$ and has no limit at $0$.
