research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1043-## Refutation
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1044-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1045-**Proof technique:** direct.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1046-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1047-1.1 The point $1$ lies in $A = (0,\infty)$ and is a limit point of $A$: given a real $\varepsilon > 0$, let $\rho$ be the smaller of $\varepsilon$ and $1$, so $\rho > 0$; then $1 + \rho/2 > 1 > 0$ lies in $A$ and satisfies $0 < |(1 + \rho/2) - 1| = \rho/2 < \varepsilon$. [L2, L3, L5]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1048-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1049-1.2 $f$ is well defined on $A$: every $x \in A$ has $x > 0$, hence $x \ne 0$ and $x^{-1}$ exists, with $x^{-1} > 0$. [L4, L7]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1050-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1051-2.1 The limit of $f$ at $1$ exists and equals $1$. Let $\varepsilon > 0$ be an arbitrary real and let $\delta$ be the smaller of $1/2$ and $\varepsilon/2$, so $\delta > 0$. For $x \in A$ with $0 < |x - 1| < \delta$ we get $x > 1 - 1/2 = 1/2 > 0$, hence $0 < 1/x < 2$ by [L4]; and $|1/x - 1| = |(1 - x)/x| = |x - 1| \cdot (1/x) < \delta \cdot 2 \le \varepsilon$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L1, L3, L4, L5]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1052-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1053-2.2 The image $f(A)$ has no upper bound. Let $M$ be an arbitrary real; by [L6] fix a natural $n \ge 1$ with $M < n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}}$, and note $n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}} > 0$. Then $x := (n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}})^{-1}$ satisfies $x > 0$, so $x \in A$, and $f(x) = x^{-1} = n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}} > M$. So no real bounds $f(A)$ above, and $f(A)$ is not bounded. [step 1.2, L4, L6, L7]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1054-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md:1055:3.1 So $f$ has a limit at the limit point $c = 1$ of its domain and is unbounded on that domain: the claim is false, while [[lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness]] remains true and gives boundedness on $A \cap N^{*}_{1/2}(1)$, where indeed $0 < f(x) < 2$ by step 2.1. [step 2.1, step 2.2, L7] ∎
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1056-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1057-## Remarks
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1058-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1059-- **The limit hypothesis is entirely local and the conclusion asked for is global**, so no argument could bridge them. The witness makes that concrete by putting the unbounded behaviour at the *other* end of the domain, arbitrarily far from $c$ in the only sense available here.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1060-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1061-- **The sequential analogue is true**, and that contrast is worth noting: a convergent sequence *is* bounded ([[lem-convergent-implies-bounded]]), because a sequence has only finitely many terms outside any tail, and finitely many reals are bounded. A function has no such structure: the part of $A$ outside a punctured neighbourhood of $c$ can be infinite and can carry arbitrary values.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1062-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1063-- **A bounded version does hold with an extra hypothesis**: if $A$ is itself contained in a punctured neighbourhood of $c$ on which the limit estimate applies, then local and global boundedness coincide. That is a hypothesis on the domain, not a theorem about limits.
