research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1464-## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1465-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1466-**Given:** A set $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, a limit point $c$ of $A$, functions $f, g : A \to \mathbb{R}$, a real $\alpha$, and reals $L, M$ with $\lim_{x \to c} f(x) = L$ and $\lim_{x \to c} g(x) = M$; for claim 4 also $M \ne 0$ and $A_0 := \{\, x \in A : g(x) \ne 0 \,\}$ ([[def-function-limit]], [[def-limit-point-r]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1467-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1468-[L1] The limit condition: $\lim_{x \to c} h(x) = P$ means that for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a real $\delta > 0$ such that every $x$ in the domain of $h$ with $0 < |x - c| < \delta$ satisfies $|h(x) - P| < \varepsilon$ ([[def-function-limit]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1469-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1470-[L2] Absolute value: $|u| \ge 0$; $|u| = 0$ if and only if $u = 0$; $|uv| = |u|\,|v|$; and $|{-u}| = |u|$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1471-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1472-[L3] Triangle inequality: $|u + v| \le |u| + |v|$ ([[lem-of-triangle-inequality]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1473-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1474-[L4] Order and field arithmetic in $\mathbb{R}$: adding two strict inequalities ([[lem-of-add-order]]); for $t > 0$, $u < v$ is equivalent to $ut < vt$, and $0 \le u \le v$ with $0 \le s \le t$ gives $us \le vt$ ([[lem-of-sign-rules]]); positive elements have positive inverses and $0 < a < b$ gives $0 < 1/b < 1/a$ ([[lem-of-inverse-positive]]); $0 < 1$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]]), so $2 > 0$ and $t/2 > 0$ for $t > 0$; inverses and the field identities ([[def-field]]); trichotomy and totality, so of finitely many positive reals the smallest is positive ([[def-ordered-field]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1475-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md:1476:[L5] Local boundedness: there are a real $\delta_0 > 0$ and a real $K \ge 0$ with $|f(x)| \le K$ for every $x \in A$ satisfying $0 < |x - c| < \delta_0$ ([[lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1477-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1478-[L6] Sign preservation: if $M \ne 0$ there is a real $\delta_s > 0$ with $|g(x)| > |M|/2 > 0$ for every $x \in A$ satisfying $0 < |x - c| < \delta_s$, and $c$ is a limit point of $A_0$ ([[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1479-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1480-[L7] Restriction: if $B \subseteq A$ has $c$ as a limit point and $\lim_{x \to c} f(x) = L$, then $\lim_{x \to c} f|_B(x) = L$ (claim 2 of [[lem-limit-is-local]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1481-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1482-[L8] Neighbourhoods ([[def-neighbourhood-r]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1483-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1484-## Proof
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1485-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1486-**Proof technique:** direct.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1487-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1488-1.1 **Sum.** Let $\varepsilon > 0$ be an arbitrary real. By [L1] fix reals $\delta_1, \delta_2 > 0$ with $|f(x) - L| < \varepsilon/2$ for every $x \in A$ satisfying $0 < |x - c| < \delta_1$ and $|g(x) - M| < \varepsilon/2$ for every $x \in A$ satisfying $0 < |x - c| < \delta_2$, and let $\delta$ be the smaller of the two, so $\delta > 0$. For $x \in A$ with $0 < |x - c| < \delta$ we get $|(f+g)(x) - (L+M)| = |(f(x) - L) + (g(x) - M)| \le |f(x) - L| + |g(x) - M| < \varepsilon$. As $\varepsilon$ was arbitrary, the limit of $f + g$ at $c$ exists and equals $L + M$: claim 1. [L1, L2, L3, L4, choose]
--
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1496-2.1 **Product.** Let $\varepsilon > 0$ be an arbitrary real. By [L1] fix reals $\delta_1, \delta_2 > 0$ with $|g(x) - M| < \varepsilon/(2K')$ on $A \cap N^{*}_{\delta_1}(c)$ and $|f(x) - L| < \varepsilon / \bigl(2(|M| + 1)\bigr)$ on $A \cap N^{*}_{\delta_2}(c)$, and let $\delta$ be the smallest of $\delta_0, \delta_1, \delta_2$, which is positive. For $x \in A$ with $0 < |x - c| < \delta$, $|f(x)g(x) - LM| = |f(x)(g(x) - M) + M(f(x) - L)| \le |f(x)|\,|g(x) - M| + |M|\,|f(x) - L| \le K'\,|g(x) - M| + (|M|+1)\,|f(x) - L| < \varepsilon/2 + \varepsilon/2 = \varepsilon$. As $\varepsilon$ was arbitrary, the limit of $fg$ at $c$ exists and equals $LM$: claim 3. [step 1.3, L1, L2, L3, L4, L8, choose]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1497-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1498-2.2 **Reciprocal.** Assume $M \ne 0$ and let $\varepsilon > 0$ be an arbitrary real. By [L1] fix a real $\delta_3 > 0$ with $|g(x) - M| < \varepsilon |M|^2 / 2$ on $A \cap N^{*}_{\delta_3}(c)$, and let $\delta$ be the smaller of $\delta_s$ and $\delta_3$. For $x \in A_0$ with $0 < |x - c| < \delta$ we have $|g(x)| > |M|/2 > 0$, hence $|g(x)|\,|M| > |M|^2/2 > 0$ and so $1/(|g(x)|\,|M|) < 2/|M|^2$; therefore $\bigl| 1/g(x) - 1/M \bigr| = |M - g(x)| \big/ \bigl(|g(x)|\,|M|\bigr) < (\varepsilon |M|^2/2)\cdot(2/|M|^2) = \varepsilon$. As $\varepsilon$ was arbitrary, the limit of $(1/g)|_{A_0}$ at $c$ exists and equals $1/M$. [step 1.4, L1, L2, L4, L8, choose]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1499-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1500-2.3 **The numerator on the smaller domain.** Assume $M \ne 0$. Since $A_0 \subseteq A$ and $c$ is a limit point of $A_0$ by step 1.4, [L7] gives that the limit of $f|_{A_0}$ at $c$ exists and equals $L$. [step 1.4, L7]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1501-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1502-3.1 **Quotient.** Assume $M \ne 0$. On the domain $A_0$, which has $c$ as a limit point, the two functions $f|_{A_0}$ and $(1/g)|_{A_0}$ have limits $L$ and $1/M$ at $c$ by steps 2.3 and 2.2, and their product is $(f/g)|_{A_0}$ by the field identities; so claim 3, applied on the domain $A_0$, gives that the limit of $(f/g)|_{A_0}$ at $c$ exists and equals $L \cdot (1/M) = L/M$. [step 2.1, step 2.2, step 2.3, L2, L4]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1503-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1504-4.1 Claims 1 to 4 are proved, each directly from the $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ definition and none of them through a sequence. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1505-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1506-## Remarks
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1507-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md:1508:- **The product estimate in one line.** The identity $fg - LM = f\,(g - M) + M\,(f - L)$ turns the problem into two products, one with a factor that is merely bounded near $c$ (that is $f$, and [[lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness]] is what bounds it) and one with a constant factor. The two constants $K'$ and $|M| + 1$ are used in place of $K$ and $|M|$ only so that they are strictly positive and may be divided by; that is the sole reason for adding $1$.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1509-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1510-- **The reciprocal estimate in one line.** The identity $1/g - 1/M = (M - g)/(gM)$ turns the problem into a numerator that is small and a denominator that must be kept away from $0$; the lower bound $|g| > |M|/2$ from [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] does exactly that, and gives the working factor $2/|M|^2$.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1511-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1512-- **Nothing here extends to $\pm\infty$.** The statement is about a finite limit point $c$ and finite values $L, M$; [[def-limits-at-infinity]] introduces limits at and to infinity, but no algebra of such limits is proved in this library, and none may be assumed. The companion page's limit at $+\infty$ is computed by a direct estimate for precisely that reason.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1513-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1514-- **The sequential analogue** is [[thm-algebra-of-limits]]. Neither implies the other for free: this theorem is about a function on a subset of $\mathbb{R}$ and is proved from $\varepsilon$ and $\delta$; that one is about sequences.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-1515-````
